1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,696
This tutorial reads a tenant's account the
way an

2
00:00:03,696 --> 00:00:06,981
accountant does, then ties it to the
books.

3
00:00:06,981 --> 00:00:10,267
As the Accountant, open Tenant Ledger,
choose Omar

4
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:13,552
Haddad and the year to 31 August 2026.

5
00:00:13,552 --> 00:00:18,069
Miftah shows one block for each account
the tenant has touched,

6
00:00:18,069 --> 00:00:22,176
and one row for each entry, with a running
balance.

7
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:23,819
Start with Rent Receivable.

8
00:00:23,819 --> 00:00:27,925
Posting the contract debited 77,000, the
rent and the deposit.

9
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:30,800
Each of the five cheques then credited

10
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:33,675
it back, so the balance is zero.

11
00:00:33,675 --> 00:00:36,960
That is normal: while the cheques are in

12
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,835
hand, the tenant owes nothing on paper.

13
00:00:39,835 --> 00:00:41,477
Now choose Layla Nasser.

14
00:00:41,477 --> 00:00:45,584
Her second cheque came back from the bank
in February.

15
00:00:45,584 --> 00:00:48,459
The return debits Rent Receivable 15,000
again.

16
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:52,976
A 500 penalty is added, and its own cheque
covers it.

17
00:00:52,976 --> 00:00:57,493
Two replacement cheques of 7,500 bring the
balance back to zero.

18
00:00:57,493 --> 00:01:02,421
Further down, Advance Rent still holds
12,328.76: billed, but not yet earned.

19
00:01:02,421 --> 00:01:06,117
PDC Receivable is the paper you hold, and
all

20
00:01:06,117 --> 00:01:09,813
of Layla's cheques have cleared, so it is
zero.

21
00:01:09,813 --> 00:01:13,099
Select a document number to open its
journal.

22
00:01:13,099 --> 00:01:15,563
The return, CBR-26/1, debits Rent
Receivable

23
00:01:15,563 --> 00:01:18,027
15,000 and credits PDC Receivable 15,000.

24
00:01:18,027 --> 00:01:22,133
Every journal balances, and none is ever
edited, only reversed.

25
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:25,008
Now open the General Ledger on Rent

26
00:01:25,008 --> 00:01:27,472
Receivable for the Residences, for
February.

27
00:01:27,472 --> 00:01:31,168
This is the control account: every
tenant's entries together.

28
00:01:31,168 --> 00:01:36,096
It closes February at 15,000 debit,
exactly Layla's balance on that day.

29
00:01:36,096 --> 00:01:39,792
The tenant ledgers add up to the control
account.

30
00:01:39,792 --> 00:01:43,899
Finally, open the Trial Balance as of 31
August 2026.

31
00:01:43,899 --> 00:01:47,595
Total debits equal total credits,
4,150,891.24 on each side.

32
00:01:47,595 --> 00:01:50,880
Filter to one building, and it still
balances.
